3.6 Error Analysis
Syntax Error
One possible syntax error would be forgetting a semicolon:
int temperature = 25

The correct statement is:
int temperature = 25;

This is a syntax error because the statement does not follow the grammatical rules of the C language. The compiler will detect the problem.
Semantic Error
A possible semantic error would be using the wrong formula, for example:
index = 100 + temperatureDeviation + turbidityPenalty;

instead of:
index = 100 - (temperatureDeviation + turbidityPenalty);

The program may compile successfully, but it produces an incorrect result. This makes it a semantic/logical error.
3.7 Compilation Lifecycle
The C program goes through several stages before becoming an executable program.
Stage	Input	Output
Preprocessing	C source code	Expanded source code
Compilation	Preprocessed source	Assembly code
Assembly	Assembly code	Object file
Linking	Object file + libraries	Executable program


The preprocessor handles directives such as #include. The compiler converts the processed C code into assembly instructions. The assembler converts the assembly instructions into machine-level object code. Finally, the linker combines the object code with required libraries to create the executable program.
4. Question 2 – Mobile Money Transaction Processing
4.1 Objective
Question 2 required the development of a simple mobile-money transaction system for an agent.
The program allows the user to:
